at just makes  them a potentially more meaningful  competitor jetblue would also benefit  from the type of pilots the deal brings  jetblue and spirit both fly mostly  airbus aircraft to have pilots who are  already type rated and qualified to fly  an airbus a320 or an a321  um simplifies things and really reduces  cost for a jetblue  next up fleet  without its deal for spirit jetblue has  a total of 282 jets in its fleet putting  it well behind the big four getting  planes it's just difficult for airlines  right now there's delays and deliveries  manufacturing delays um so just the idea  of just quickly acquiring a couple  hundred planes was very enticing for  jetblue so acquiring spirits all airbus  fleet would give jetblue a total of 458  planes jetblue says with some 300 more  on order it would take it many years i  think they've said eight years to sort  of get to that size that they need to  get organically so  they kind of looked around and deciding  really the best way to kind of jump to a  size that can even begin to compete with  these these bigger airlines is through  an acquisition these planes won't fly as  is though spirit's yellow will be  painted over and jetblue has said it  would take seats off spirit's packed  planes to bring them in line with  jetblue's roomier model  but even taking seats away this deal  would bring jetblue thousands more seats  total  so let's talk about seats  to see how the airline stacks up it's  best to look at a metric known as  available seat miles this metric is  calculated by multiplying the number of  seats available on a given flight by the  number of miles that plane will travel  the more asms you have the more capacity  you have so it's a great metric to show  the size from a capacity standpoint of  an airline in 2021 jetblue had 54  billion available seat miles combining  with spirits 40 billion would edge the  airline closer to 4th place southwest  than 6th place alaska  but even with spirit jetblue's slice of  overall seat capacity will sit at 8  percent analysts say to grow that  jetblue will need to expand its reach i  think what really is needed for jetblue  to get its share  um closer to that of southwest  united delta in america is to set up  some sort of a focus city  with material size in the middle of the  country and lastly destinations  currently jetblue flies to 98  destinations spirit flies to 90. the  combined airline wouldn't fly to 188  however that number is more like 127  according to jetblue often in airline  mergers in the past you know regulators  say well this is where you overlap we  need you to cut certain um  you know gates at airports or you know  need you to divest some assets in order  to so so there's not so much overlap if  the deal is allowed to go forward there  will likely be some pruning at least  127 destinations would move jetblue just  above southwest which currently flies to  121 locations the other three would all  have more than double jetblue's  destinations  the deal would make fort lauderdale  jetblue's busiest airport according to a  cerium analysis the combined airline  would then surpass southwest and delta  to become florida's second busiest  carrier by seats after american florida  has been especially the last couple of  years just one of the busiest places in  the country  to fly it is important to airlines to  have a meaningful presence there and  growing there can be really advantageous  for them jetblue says its larger  presence in cities like fort lauderdale  would drive down prices for flyers  however jetblue would be taking an ultra  low cost carrier out of the equation and  some argue that less competition could  end up raising prices for all  proving that competition and low prices  will remain after the deal will be key  to getting regulators to bless the  merger jetblue has argued that the  government has allowed the creation of  four really big airlines that dominate  the market by allowing all these mergers  and tha